  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1134 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge which has been our main weather influence in \nthe past few days will continue to move slowly east into the Lower \nMississippi Valley on Sunday. To our west an upper low will move \nfrom the Arizona/Utah border to northeastern Utah, and a trough will \nextend south from this low along the NM/AZ border by Sunday evening. \nShowers and a few thunderstorms associated with this system will \naffect western and far northwestern parts of Texas on Sunday. A few \nshowers may develop a little farther east and affect our western \ncounties Sunday afternoon and evening, mainly west of a Sweetwater \nto Sterling City to Ozona line. For the rest of our area, mostly \ncloudy skies in the morning will become partly cloudy in the \nafternoon. Highs will be mostly 76-81 degrees with a south to \nsoutheast breeze. \n\nThe aforementioned upper low will open into a wave as the system \nlifts northeast into the central High Plains on Monday. A shortwave \ntrough is forecast to move northeast into our area on Monday, \npossibly followed by another disturbance Tuesday. With fairly moist \nsouth-southeast low-level flow and precipitable water values at or \nabove 1 inch, our area will have a possibility of isolated to \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms. Expect a reduced diurnal \ntemperature range with considerable cloud cover, and highs Monday \nand Tuesday are expected to be mostly in the mid to upper 70s. \n\nConsiderable cloud cover is expected again Wednesday with moist \nsouth-southeast low-level flow, but with a lack of upper support and \nsurface focus, our area should be free of rain chances. The \ndeterministic medium range models differ on the details of how the \npattern will evolve late next week. But model ensembles support the \nreturn of rain chances Thursday into Friday, when southwest flow \naloft develops over our area ahead of a large scale trough \ndeveloping along the West Coast. Highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s \nWednesday will trend down to the lower and middle 70s by Friday. \nNighttime lows will be mild and above normal, in the mid 50s to \naround 60.",
